Original commit body from 15a5975aedf0ded9914497adc59ff65d31f1f8d7:

iter_axis_set_assignments collected string-literal sets and frozenset()
wrappers only, so a dict, tuple or list copy of any of the four axis
vocabularies was invisible to every drift linter at once. It now also
collects bare tuples/lists, dict keys, dict values, and elements that are
module-local NAME references to string constants.

A dict with strings on both sides yields <NAME>:keys and <NAME>:values
separately. io_boundary._READ_TARGET_KIND_BOUNDARY is keyed by
io_target_kind values and valued by io-boundary names -- two axes in one
assignment -- so unioning the sides would force io-boundary to exclude the
whole constant to silence the keys and lose the check on the values. A
bare-name exclusion still silences both sides.

The mandatory report-only pass corrected the item's diagnosis: the three
derived io-boundary constants are produced by a CALL and should stay
invisible, because a constant derived from the registry cannot drift from
it; AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP and DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS fail the NAME
FILTER rather than the shape test and are filed separately.

Five newly-red constants are axis-name collisions, each 0/N conformant and
each excluded with its own reason. The sixth, scip/index.py's _KIND_MAP, is
a real Symbol.kind consumer at 5/8 conformant whose other values are
phantoms; filed as INV-lagot and suppressed with an exclusion that says so.

